      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks that makes sense. one thing that's still tripping me up is how to talk about the 'uncertainty' or 'noise' in the data. like, when the confidence interval is wider, it feels like there's something hidden or just messy in the background making it hard to be precise. how should i academically frame that 'unpredictability' in my discussion section so it doesn't sound like i just have bad data?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely right to be concerned about framing that \"messy\" feeling correctly, because in sociology, data is rarely clean, and a wide confidence interval often just means your sample is small or your effect is subtle, not that your research is flawed. Instead of calling it \"noise\" or \"bad data,\" you should frame it as **estimation uncertainty** or **sampling variability**, which are natural features of any statistical study.\n\nThink of the confidence interval width as a measure of how much the \"truth\" might shift if you were to repeat your experiment with a different group of people. A wider interval simply means your current sample hasn't pinned down the exact value with high precision yet, but it doesn't invalidate your findings.\n\nHere is how you can academically frame this in your discussion section:\n\nInstead of saying, \"The data is too noisy to be precise,\" try phrasing it like this:\n> \"The relatively wide 95% confidence interval [0.12, 0.85] reflects the inherent **sampling variability** associated with our sample size. While this indicates that the precise magnitude of the effect remains uncertain, it does not diminish the statistical significance of the finding. Rather, it highlights",
